    {
      "id": "dff671d3-6056-48b7-a299-f38e67334855",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -1344,
        4016
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 560,
        "height": 1280,
        "content": "## WooCommerce Blog-Pinecone\n\nSyncs WordPress/WooCommerce **static pages and blog posts** into a Pinecone vector index, so an AI assistant can search them. It handles the initial load and then keeps the index up to date: new and changed items are re-indexed, and items deleted from WordPress are removed.\n\n### How one run works\n\n1. Fetches a lightweight list of published pages and posts (id + modification date only)\n2. Compares it with the deduplication table and selects the first `staticBatchSize` items that are new or changed\n3. Fetches the FULL content only for that batch\n4. For each item: formats the text, deletes its old chunks in Pinecone, creates OpenAI embeddings and saves the new chunks - one item at a time\n5. Removes from Pinecone (and from the table) items that no longer exist in WordPress\n6. Ends on its own - the next run picks up the next batch\n\n### Setup steps\n\n1. Connect the credentials: WooCommerce API, OpenAI, Pinecone\n2. Create a Pinecone index (dimension 1536, the default OpenAI embedding size)\n3. Create a Data Table named `indexed_wc_blog_v1` with three text columns: `productId`, `indexedAt`, `dateModified`\n4. Fill in the \"Set Static Content Settings\" node:\n   - `wooBaseUrl` - your store's address, e.g. https://your-store.com\n   - `wooCurrency` - not used here (pages and posts have no prices)\n   - `pineconeIndexName`, `pineconeIndexHost` - from the Pinecone console\n   - `pineconeNamespace` - \"default\" is fine\n   - `staticBatchSize` - how many items one run processes\n5. Start with a small `staticBatchSize` (e.g. 2-5) and run the manual trigger to check the result\n6. Run it several times, or let the schedule (default: daily at 4:00 AM) finish the initial load\n\n### Limits and safety\n\n- Reads up to 100 pages and 100 posts. On larger sites, items beyond the first 100 of each type are not indexed.\n- If WordPress returns an error, the run stops and nothing is deleted. Nothing is deleted either when a list is empty or has reached the 100-item limit.\n\n### Customization\n\nChange the chunk size (Split Content Text), the embedding model, the Pinecone namespace or the schedule."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
